.TH FSCK.FILSYS 1 "August 2026" "filsys 1.0.0" "File Format and Disk Utilities"
.SH NAME
fsck.filsys \- filesystem storage and directory consistency check
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B fsck.filsys
.RB [ \-v
.IR <4|5|6|7> ]
.RB [ \-o
.IR block ]
.I image
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B fsck.filsys
checks a Research Unix filesystem inside a disk image.  It validates the
superblock; walks the free-block list checking for out-of-range or duplicated
blocks and a non-terminating chain; and walks the i-list checking each inode's
block pointers.
.PP
The Seventh Edition had no single
.I fsck
command: storage consistency was checked with
.BR icheck (8)
(which built a bit map of used blocks and compared it against the free list)
and directory consistency with
.BR dcheck (8)
(which compared each inode's link count with the number of directory entries
that reference it).  The
.I fsck.filsys
name is the modern
.BR fsck (8)
convention; the two checks are folded together and its report carries the
same findings the V7 pair reported (duplicate blocks, bad blocks, blocks
outside the filesystem, and link-count/disagreement diagnostics).
.PP
The edition is selected with
.BR \-v :
4, 5, or 6 select the Sixth Edition format (V4 and V5 are byte-identical to
V6 on disk); 7 selects Seventh Edition.  The default is 7.  A Research Unix
disk image holds one or more filesystems
.RI ( partitions ),
selected by a partition table compiled into the kernel, not stored on the
disk.  By default
.B fsck.filsys
checks the filesystem at block 0 (the first partition); the
.B \-o
option checks a filesystem at a different block offset.
.PP
The exit status is 0 if the filesystem is clean, 1 if errors were found, and 2
on a usage error.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-v " <4|5|6|7>"
Filesystem edition.  4, 5 and 6 are byte-identical (the V6 format); 7 is V7.
Default 7.  A leading
.B v
is accepted (v6, v7, ...).
.TP
.BI \-o " block"
Check the filesystem at
.I block
(512-byte blocks) within the image, instead of block 0.
.SH EXAMPLE
.nf
fsck.filsys image.dk                # check the filesystem at block 0 (V7)
fsck.filsys -v 6 image.dk           # check a V6 (V4/V5) filesystem
fsck.filsys -o 18392 image.dk       # check the filesystem at block 18392
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR mkfs.filsys (1),
.BR findfs.filsys (1),
.BR mount.filsys (1),
.BR filsys (5),
.BR icheck (8),
.BR dcheck (8)
